<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Song of Australia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>&quot; was the result of a competition for a &quot;patriotic song&quot; held by the Gawler Institute in 1859. On 1 October the South Australian Register announced a prize of ten guineas, open to South Australia, for the words of a song to be entitled &quot;The Song of Australia&quot;, the copyright of the winning words to pass to the Institute; entries were to be sent unsigned, under a motto, to George Isaacs at Gawler.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>The Song of Australia</b>&quot; was the result of a competition for a &quot;patriotic song&quot; held by the Gawler Institute in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-australia">The Song of Australia</a></li></ul></section>
